[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said one issue, one fix queued. Its own cause table already showed a
second cause, and its footnote hid a third. Each one needs its own fix, so each has its own ticket.
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed and booking-v3 is retired. The third
problem is still open.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="https://github.com/zalio-pl/mega-support-kb" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, shift: 3 }} states={{ parser: 'fixed', hint: "won't fix", shift: 'open' }} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 did not close <code>shift</code>. One-day moves keep coming until a time-zone fix
ships.
</Callout>

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant writes the day
  first. Day and month swap: 7 April arrives as 4 July. Fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
  locales`.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants
  type month first, as told, and the form reads it day first. It looks like the parser bug, but
  the fix belongs in the form. Won't fix: booking-v3 is retired.
- **`shift`**: no swap. The date moves one day earlier (Tuesday shows as Monday, Lisbon gets
  Tuesday and Toronto gets Monday). Most likely a time zone or DST conversion. The desk had
  already read these reports as one-day moves. Open: neither change above touches it.

## How the reports were placed

- Each report is counted once, by id. The ticket export repeats some rows.
- The tenant decides the cause, not the reporter's browser. Intake's tag is a guess and is
  ignored here (see `causes.ts`).
- The desk's one-day reading (`desk-notes.json`) marks the `shift` reports. They stay counted under
  their tenant's cause too, as the desk counts them.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. All three
problems produce the same subjects ("wrong day", "date is wrong"), so subject matching cannot keep
them apart. Splitting them needs the tenant, the channel and the body.

